Keywords: Inorganic perovskite solar cells; carbon electrode; hole-transporting layer 1 Introduction
Over the past several years, the conversion efficiency of organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has rapidly increased from $3.8\%$ to as high as $23.3\%$ due to continuous improvement in the composition of perovskite materials, the device structure, and the fabrication techniques [1-5], rendering it a promising alternative to commercialized Si-based solar cells. Unfortunately, the stability of organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite materials is very poor owing to the rapid degradation of their organic component under the moisture and thermal stress, which make them intrinsically unsuitable for practical application [6-8]. To completely replace the organic components by an inorganic cation (such as cesium cation) can remarkably improve the stability of perovskite materials. Therefore, all inorganic cesium lead halide $\mathrm{(Cs{Pb}{X_{3}}}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathrm{X=I}$ , Br, and Cl) perovskite materials have been attracting widespread attention as a stable light absorber for photovoltaic application [9-18].
Among $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ inorganic perovskite materials, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ has been considered as a promising photovoltaic light absorber for PSCs due to its reasonable bandgap of 1.92 eV and high structural stability in cubic phase [19-25]. Beal et al reported a CsPbI2Br inorganic PSC fabricated by a solution-processed method with a stabilized power conversion efficiency of $6.5\%$ [26]. The band alignment engineering by profiling interface was performed to enhance the conversion efficiency of CsPbI2Br PSC to $12.39\%$ [27]. Yan et al. and Liu et al. reported that the optimization of the charge transporting layer for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs suppressed the interface charge recombination and enhanced the charge extraction efficiency, and the conversion efficiencies over $13\%$ were achieved [28,29]. Nevertheless, these high-efficiency $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs generally employed $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis(N.N-di- $p$ -methoxyphenylamine)- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiroOMeTAD) or poly(triarlyamine) (PTAA) as the organic hole transporting layer (HTL), which need to be doped with lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulphonyl)imide (Li-TFSI) in order to increase their hole mobility. It is reported that $\mathfrak{a}$ -phase CsPbI2Br perovskite is stable to light, oxygen, and heat, but sensitive to humidity [30-32]. Li-TFSI is hydroscopic and deliquescent, which is detrimental to the stability of CsPbI2Br PSCs because Li-TFSI enhances the moisture ingress of HTL and remarkably accelerates the moisture-induced phase transition of CsPbI2Br perovskite. This highlights the importance of developing hydrophobic HTL with high conductivity for CsPbI2Br inorganic PSCs.
Poly(3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT) is a p-type conductive polymer with high stability and hydrophobicity. P3HT film can be deposited using solution-processing method due to its dissolubility in different solvents. Moreover, hybrid composites of P3HT and functional inorganic nanomaterials usually achieve the combined property of each component, such as conductive and mechanical properties [33]. These characteristics make P3HT a promising material for efficient thin-film solar cells [34]. Furthermore, multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) have been considered as the outstanding additives for improving the charge transport within the polymer matrices [35]. Herein, we develop a stable and high-performance carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br inorganic PSC using P3HT-MWCNT composite as the HTL. The compact P3HT-MWCNT film has a protective effect on $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ layer by shielding it from the ambient moisture and preventing the moisture ingress, which greatly enhances the stability of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ inorganic PSCs. Moreover, MWCNTs greatly enhance the charge transport of P3HT matrix while P3HT prohibits the electron transfer from $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ layer to MWCNTs. In addition, stable and low-cost carbon is employed in this work as the back electrode of PSCs, avoiding the use of noble metal (such as $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ and Au). As a result, the as-prepared carbon electrode-based $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ inorganic PSC achieves a conversion efficiency up to $10.01\%$ and displays an excellent long-term stability.
2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) (75 wt% in isopropanol), N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ ), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.8\%$ ), and chlorobenzene $(99.9\%)$ were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}_{2}}$ $(99.9\%)$ , $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ $(99.9\%)$ , and CsI $(99.9\%)$ were purchased from Alfa Aesar. P3HT $\mathrm{\DeltaM_{w}=10000-100000)}$ was purchased from Polymer Light Corp.
2.2 Fabrication of carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br inorganic PSCs
Fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) glass substrates were cleaned by ultrasonication using deionized water, acetone, and isopropanol. Thereafter, they were treated in a UV$\mathrm{O}_{3}$ system for 15 min. A compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was deposited on a pre-cleaned FTO glass by spin-coating an ethanol solution of titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) 1 $(0.15\mathrm{M})$ at $4500\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for 45 s and sintered at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was then deposited by spin-coating a mixed solution of a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ paste (Dyesol 18 NRD) and ethanol (with the weight ratio of 1:9) on the compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer at $4000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{s}$ and annealed at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ .
$0.147\mathrm{\g\PbBr}_{2}$ , $0.184\mathrm{~g~PbI}_{2}$ , and $0.208\ \mathrm{g}$ CsI were dissolved in the mixed solvent of N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) with the volume ratio of 4:1 to prepare CsPbI2Br precursor solution. The CsPbI2Br film was deposited onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer by spin-coating the as-prepared precursor solution at $1000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 5 s and then $3500\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30{\mathrm{~s}}.$ . The films were subsequently heated at $280^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ .
P3HT layer was deposited on $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ film by spin-coating a P3HT/chlorobenzene solution with a concentration of $16~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ at $2000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s and subsequently heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min. Similarly, P3HT-MWCNT composite layer was deposited on $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ film by spin-coating P3HT-MWCNT/chlorobenzene solution ( $\mathrm{16~mg}$ P3HT and $8~\mathrm{mg}$ MWCNT in 1 mL chlorobenzene, MWCNT/P3HT $ =2{:}4$ (in weight)) at 2000 rpm for 30 s. MWCNTs with diameters of $10{-}15~\mathrm{nm}$ and lengths of $10{-}30~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. The electrical conductivity of MWCNTs is around 150 S $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . Finally, the carbon electrode was deposited by doctor-blade coating of carbon paste. The carbon paste was prepared by grinding $\mathbf{0.5\textrm{g}}$ carbon black, $\boldsymbol{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ graphite, $0.7\mathrm{mL}$ alpha-terpineol, and $0.05\mathrm{mL}$ titanium isopropoxide in an agate mortar.
## 2.3 Characterization and measurements
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S-4800) was used to observe the morphology characteristics of the samples. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded by a Rigaku Smartlab X-ray diffractometer with Cu Kα radiation. The absorption spectra were measured by a UV-Vis spectrophotometer (UV-3000). The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) measurements were performed on an F900 fluorescence spectrophotometer with an excitation wavelength of 405 nm.
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were carried out on a VersaSTAT3 electrochemical system (AMETEK) over the frequency range from $10^{6}$ to 1 Hz. The current density-voltage curves were recorded by a computer-controlled Keithley 2400 source meter under a solar simulator illumination of $100\ \mathrm{mW\cm^{-2}}$ (AM 1.5). The cells were masked with an active area of $0.12\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The light density was calibrated with a c-Si reference cell. The incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) was measured by using a 150 W Xe lamp equipped with a monochromator. All the measurements were performed in air.
3 Results and discussion
