Fig. S1a exhibits the schematic architecture and energy band diagram of the carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSC investigated in this work, where the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, CsPbI2Br layer, P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL, and carbon electrode are sequentially deposited onto FTO substrate. For comparison, the carbon electrode-based PSCs with pure P3HT HTL and without HTL (their schematic architecture are shown in Figs. S1b and 1c) are also fabricated. Figs. 1a-c show top-view SEM images of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ layer deposited onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, P3HT and P3HT/MWCNT composite layers deposited onto CsPbI2Br film.
Fig. 1a shows a dense and smooth CsPbI2Br perovskite film with good coverage on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. SEM images in Figs. 1b and c indicate that the compact P3HT and P3HTMWCNT layers fully cover the CsPbI2Br film. This can shield the CsPbI2Br perovskite film from the ambient moisture and prevents direct contact between CsPbI2Br film and carbon electrode. In addition, Fig. 1c indicates that MWCNTs disperse uniformly in P3HT matrix. P3HT prohibits MWCNTs from being in direct contact with CsPbI2Br perovskite layer and then prevents the electron transferring from CsPbI2Br layer to MWCNTs, resulting in the selective hole transfer to MWCNTs (see Fig. S1). MWCNTs embedded in P3HT layer can greatly improve the charge transport of P3HT due to the exceptional charge transport characteristics of MWCNTs and the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction between MWCNTs and P3HT [36].
P3HT-MWCNT layer (a); XRD curves of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film stored in ambient air without (b) and with P3HT (c) and P3HT-MWCNT layer (d).
Fig. 2 displays the photographs and XRD patterns of CsPbI2Br film without and with P3HT and P3HT-MWCNT composite layers for various time stored in ambient air 1 $(\sim35\%$ R.H.). Because the $\mathsf{a}{\mathrm{-CsPbI}}_{2}\mathsf{B r}$ film is sensitive to the humidity, the pristine $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ perovskite film shows a remarkable transition from black $\upalpha$ -phase to yellow δ- phase within $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , as shown in Figs. 2a, 2b, and Fig. S2. However, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ perovskite film capped with P3HT layer displays a greatly enhanced ambient stability. From Figs. 2a and 2c, no obvious color and crystal structure changes are observed for the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ perovskite film capped with P3HT layer even after $120\mathrm{~h~}$ in ambient air. This can be attributed to that the hydrophobic and dense P3HT layer, which cap to CsPbI2Br perovskite film, could shield the CsPbI2Br perovskite film from the ambient moisture and then have a protective effect on the CsPbI2Br perovskite film. Incorporating MWCNTs into P3HT does not have a negative impact on the protective effect of the P3HT layer. P3HT-MWCNT composite layer can protect the CsPbI2Br perovskite film as well as the pure P3HT layer. This is verified by the photographs and XRD patterns measurements (see Figs. 2a and 2d).
Steady-state PL measurements are carried out to investigate the holes extraction behaviors at the CsPbI2Br/HTM layer interfaces. Fig. 3 shows the steady-state PL spectra of CsPbI2Br, CsPbI2Br/P3HT, and CsPbI2Br/P3HT-MWCNT films, which are deposited on silica glass. The CsPbI2Br film exhibits a PL peak at the wavelength of $655\ \mathrm{nm}$ , matching with its bandgap. An obvious PL quenching is observed when CsPbI2Br film interfaces with P3HT, demonstrating that P3HT can extract the holes from CsPbI2Br layer. The CsPbI2Br/P3HT-MWCNT film shows much stronger PL quenching than the CsPbI2Br/P3HT, suggesting a higher holes extraction capability of P3HT-MWCNT composite layer from $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ film. Fig. S3 exhibits the transient PL spectra of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ and CsPbI2Br/P3HT-MWCNT films. The PL lifetime can be determined by single-exponential fitting of the decay curves. A long lifetime of 17.9 ns is obtained for pure $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film, while CsPbI2Br/P3HT-MWCNT films display a very short lifetime of 1.04 ns. This result further demonstrates that the P3HT-MWCNT composite layer can effectively extract hole from CsPbI2Br film. In addition, the PL spectra of both CsPbI2Br/P3HT and CsPbI2Br/P3HT-MWCNT films display a blue shift of about $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ compared to that of pure CsPbI2Br film. This slight blue shift can be considered as an evidence for the surface-trap-state passivation of CsPbI2Br layer by P3HT [37,38]. It is reported that the S atoms of P3HT can bond with Cs and Pb atoms at the cesium lead halide perovskite/P3HT interface and then passivate the interfacial under-coordinated ${\mathrm{Cs}^{+}}$ ions and the anti-site defects in cesium lead halide perovskite film [39].
Fig. 4 (a) IV curves of champion carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs without HTL and with P3HT and P3HT-MWCNT composite HTLs under illumination of $100~\mathrm{{mW}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ; (b) IPCE and integrated current density of carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSC with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL; (c) stabilized power output measurement for carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSC with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL.
The photovoltaic performances of the fabricated carbon electrode-based $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs with different HTLs are measured under simulated AM 1.5 illumination ( $\mathrm{100~mW}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ). Fig. 4a shows the current density-voltage (IV) curves of carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs without HTL and with P3HT and P3HT-MWCNT composite HTLs. The photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 1. From Fig. 4a and Table 1, it can be seen that the device without HTL exhibits a poor photovoltaic performance with a short-current density $(\mathrm{J_{sc}})$ of $11.01\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , an open-circuit voltage $(\mathrm{V_{oc}})$ of $1.09\mathrm{V}$ , a fill factor (FF) of 0.46, and a conversion efficiency (η) of $5.52\%$ . This result suggests that direct contact of the carbon electrode to the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ film in PSCs is unadvantageous for selective holes extraction. The transfer of the photogenerated electrons from CsPbI2Br perovskite film to the carbon electrode is likely to occur in the device without HTL, leading to the recombination losses. The introduce of P3HT HTL can improve the holes extraction but block the electrons (as shown in Fig. S1). Accordingly, the device with P3HT HTL achieves an improved efficiency up to $7.69\%$ with a $\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of 12.17 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{oc}}$ of 1.15 V. Nevertheless, P3HT-based device displays a low FF. This can be attributed to the low charge mobility within the P3HT layer, which limits the holes extraction from the perovskite layer. Incorporating MWCNTs into P3HT matrix can remarkably enhance the charger mobility within P3HT matrix. Consequently, the champion device with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL displays a further improved photovoltaic performance with a $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{oc}}$ of $1.21{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , a $\mathrm{J_{sc}}$ of $13.35\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a FF of 0.62, and a $\boldsymbol{\upeta}$ of $10.01\%$ . From the histogram of IV scan efficiency for a batch of 30 solar cells (Fig. S4), an average efficiency of $9.36\%$ is determined for the cell with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL. Fig. 4b displays the incident photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE) spectrum of the champion device with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL. The integrated current density calculated from IPCE spectrum is about $12.96\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which is in good agreement with the $\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{sc}}$ obtained from IV curve. Fig. 4c shows the stabilized outputs of the current density and power conversion efficiency for the champion cell with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL at the maximum power point (0.91 V). The stabilized photocurrent density at the bias of 0.91 V is $9.72~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , yielding a stabilized conversion efficiency of $8.85\%$ . This improved photovoltaic performance of carbon-based CsPbI2Br PSC with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL can be attributed to the enhanced conductivity of P3HT matrix by incorporating MWCNTs, which enhances the holes extraction from $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ perovskite layer and the holes transport within the HTM layer, and then suppresses the recombination in the device [40,41]. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements are performed to investigate the interfacial charge recombination behaviors in carbon electrode-based $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs. Nyquist plots shown in Fig. S5 indicate that the recombination resistance $(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{rec}})$ of the carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs without HTL and with P3HT and P3HT-MWCNT composite HTLs are 605, 1962, and $3538~\Omega$ , respectively. The highest $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}$ for the device with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL suggests that the P3HT-MWCNT composite as the HTL is superior in reducing the charge recombination in the device. We also fabricate the large-area $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSC with P3HT-MWCNT composite HTL and explore its photovoltaic performance. As shown in Fig. 5, the cell with an active area of $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ achieves a decent conversion efficiency of $7.52\%$ . The efficiency of the large-area device is lower than that of the small-area one, which can be mainly attributed to the increased series resistance of the large-area device [42].
The weight ratio of MWCNT to P3HT for composite HTL plays a vital role in influencing the photovoltaic performance of carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs. Fig. 6 displays the current density-voltage curves of CsPbI2Br PSCs based on composite HTLs containing different contents of MWCNT. The corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table S1. The conversion efficiencies of PSCs based on composite HTLs with the MWCNT-to-P3HT weight ratios of 1:4, 2:4, and 3:4 are $8.48\%$ , $10.01\%$ , and $9.13\%$ , respectively. Clearly, the optimized MWCNT-to-P3HT weight ratio of the composite HTL for CsPbI2Br PSCs is 2:4. When the content of MWCNT in composite HTL is very low (such as the weight ratio of MWCNT to P3HT is 1:4), MWCNTs can not effectively form an interconnected conductive network through the P3HT matrice. This limits the holes transport in HTL and the holes extraction from perovskite film, leading to a low FF. However, when the content of MWCNTs in composite HTL is too high (such as the weight ratio of MWCNT to P3HT is 3:4), some MWCNTs can contact directly to perovskite film. This enhances the charge recombination in the device, leading to a decrease in the conversion efficiency.
P3HT-MWCNT composite HTLs with various weight ratios of MWCNT to P3HT. The concentration of $16\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ is fixed for P3HT.
The long-term stability of carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs is explored by monitoring the variation of the conversion efficiency with time. The as-prepared carbon electrode-based $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs were stored in ambient air $67\%$ R.H., $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) without encapsulation. Fig. 7 presents the variation of the normalized conversion efficiency of carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs with time. Obviously, the carbon electrodebased $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSC without HTL exhibits poor stability in ambient air, the conversion efficiency decreases rapidly to $20\%$ of the initial value after $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . Furthermore, we also fabricate the carbon electrode-based $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSC using 2,2,7,-7-tetrakis(N,N-di-pmethoxyphenylamine)- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) doped with Lithium bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide (Li-TFSI) as the HTL and explore its long-term stability in ambient air (see Fig. S6). The high hydrophility of Li-TFSI remarkably accelerates the phase transition of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ perovskite. The efficiency of the non-encapsulated device with Spiro-OMeTAD/Li-TFSI HTL decreases by $90\%$ after $120\ \mathrm{min}$ in the ambient air. In contrast, the conversion efficiencies of the carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs with P3HT and P3HT-MWCNT composite HTLs retain ${\sim}85\%$ of its initial value after $240\mathrm{~h~}$ in the ambient air, indicating superior long-term stability. Such enhanced stability of the carbon electrode-based CsPbI2Br PSCs with P3HT and P3HTMWCNT composite HTLs can be attributed to the protective effect of the dense and hydrophobic HTL by shielding CsPbI2Br perovskite film from the ambient moisture.
